rsz: a return controller for setup repair, no knobs

Policy candidate from the repair-knobs campaign (ideas/repair-knobs-nihl.md),
carried while it is measured. Not upstreamed. If it dominates today's
defaults on every asap7 and sky130hd design, it goes upstream as the
replacement for TNS_END_PERCENT, SKIP_LAST_GASP and SKIP_GATE_CLONING,
which stay accepted and inert.

Every knob above is a binary guess about a continuous, observable
quantity: how much the next unit of work will buy. The census shows the
same knob setting winning on one design and losing on the next, because
the return differs per design and the knob cannot see it. This reads it.

Per phase, at each window of passes (the existing hundred-pass
interval), the controller measures what the window bought in TNS and
WNS, remembers the best window the run has had, and stops the phase
after two consecutive windows below 1% of that best with WNS flat. Gains
are per window of passes, never per second: the first cut measured per
second and that made the decision, and so the result, depend on the
machine and its load. The best window is kept across the phases of one
run, so a last gasp chasing crumbs after a productive main phase stops
within two windows. The phase ends on the controller's own decision;
the first cut signalled through the legacy exit's two-consecutive
bookkeeping and on jpeg grt that made the main phase 300 passes longer
instead of shorter. Last gasp is not skipped on the main phase's
account -- measured, a main phase that stopped for lack of return still
hands over TNS that last gasp's looser acceptance finds (jpeg cts:
655 ps) -- it runs under the same controller and ends when its own
return is gone. A move type tried a hundred times in a phase with no
acceptance is retired for the rest of that phase.

Every constant is a ratio of the run's own measurements or the scale the
optimizer already uses, so nothing here is a value a user could tune per
design. The legacy fix-rate test and its thousand-pass floor are left in
place beneath it.

Chesterton's Fence: the thousand-pass floor protects a slow start, where
early windows fix little of the total TNS. The controller's reference is
the phase's own peak rate, not the initial TNS, so a slow start is simply
a low peak that later windows raise; it cannot fire until a rate has been
measured and then fallen.

